## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a practical, testable fair-use layer for a multi-tenant LLM gateway with billing considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas and burst handling using a token bucket
- Isolation of prompts and responses per tenant
- Simple REST API for quota status and consumption
- Observability and a minimal test plan for bucket behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerMs) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillPerMs = refillPerMs;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  tick() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.lastRefill;
    const refill = Math.floor(elapsed * this.refillPerMs);
    if (refill > 0) {
      this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + refill);
      this.lastRefill = now;
    }
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    this.tick();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores a new angle on LLM-ops: enforcing per-tenant privacy at the gateway with dynamic redaction policies, balancing data minimization with latency.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant redaction policy (strict/moderate/none)
- In-flight prompt redaction and post-processing of outputs
- Lightweight in-memory policy engine and fast lookups
- Privacy-preserving auditing and zero-PII logs

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPrompt(prompt, policy) {
  if (policy === 'strict') {
    return prompt
      .replace(/\b(\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4})\b/g, 'REDACTED_SSN')
      .replace(/\b\d{16}\b/g, 'REDACTED_CARD');
  }
  if (policy === 'moderate') {
    return prompt.replace(/email:\s*\S+@\S+/g, 'email=REDACTED');
  }
  return prompt;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify policy drift and retroactive redactions across model updates?
- What metrics and logs would you keep to demonstrate compliance without leaking data?

